\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Unless otherwise noted, all experiments in this section use the v1 prompt (\S\ref{sec:model}; full templates in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompts}).

\subsection{Count-Degradation Curves}
\label{sec:count_curves}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_count_curves.pdf}
\end{minipage}
\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_bcs_scaling.pdf}
\end{minipage}
\caption{\textbf{(a)} Count-degradation curves across four datasets. NER recall generally declines and RE accuracy (both FewRel and DocRED) declines monotonically with schema size; MAVEN relaxed F1 increases (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). \textbf{(b)} BCS-controlled FewRel accuracy. The BCS--accuracy slope is consistent across the independent 16-relation pool and within-pool 16-relation subsets (permutation $p = 0.60$); the wider BCS range available at 80 relations produces larger absolute gaps.}
\label{fig:main_results}
\end{figure}

We first examine how extraction performance changes as the number of types in the prompt schema increases (Experiment~1; Figure~\ref{fig:main_results}a).

\paragraph{NER (Few-NERD).}
Recall declines from 0.72 at count${}\,=\,1$ to 0.42 at count${}\,=\,64$ ($-$42\% relative), with minor non-monotonicity within sampling variability (bootstrap CIs overlap). Precision rises inversely (0.11 $\to$ 0.52), reflecting a shift from over-prediction to under-prediction as candidate types increase.

\paragraph{RE (FewRel).}
Accuracy drops monotonically from 85.7\% at count${}\,=\,1$ through 58.8\% at count${}\,=\,40$ to 50.4\% at count${}\,=\,80$, a near-halving when the model must discriminate among all 80 relation types simultaneously. (The count${}\,=\,40$ evaluation uses 40-relation subsets sampled from the 80-relation pool, not an independent schema.)

\paragraph{Document-level RE (DocRED).}
Accuracy declines from 88.0\% (count 1) to 38.7\% (count 96), a 56\% relative drop consistent with FewRel, confirming generalization to document-level RE.

\paragraph{ED (MAVEN).}
MAVEN relaxed F1 \emph{increases} from 0.106 at count${}\,=\,1$ to 0.247 at count${}\,=\,44$---a reversal explained by relaxed matching, same-domain hierarchy providing disambiguating context, and class imbalance where rare types benefit from co-presentation with related types (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}).

\paragraph{Cross-model replication.}
Channel~1 replicates on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct: $-$43\,pp recall decline on Few-NERD (vs.\ $-$34\,pp for Qwen3-32B; Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}). Per-batch correlation $r = 0.725$, indicating schema configuration drives difficulty rather than model-specific factors.

\paragraph{Summary.}
Schema size degrades NER and RE (Channel~1), but MAVEN's reversed trend shows task structure mediates the relationship. DocRED confirms generalization to document-level RE (96 types). Both channels replicate on Llama-3.1-8B (count $r = 0.725$; confusability in \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}).

\subsection{Pairwise Confusability and Type Confusion}
\label{sec:pairwise}

We test whether pairwise SCS predicts type-level confusion rates. For each type pair $(t_i, t_j)$, we compute the confusion rate---the fraction of $t_i$ gold instances misclassified as $t_j$---and correlate it with $\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)$. Table~\ref{tab:pairwise} reports Pearson correlations with bootstrap 95\% CIs.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Embed.} & \textbf{$n$ pairs} & \textbf{$r$} & \textbf{$r^2$} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{$p$} \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & Qwen3 & 1{,}953 & 0.112 & 0.013 & {[}0.076, 0.156{]} & $7{\times}10^{-7}$ \\
MAVEN & MiniLM & 11{,}026 & 0.129 & 0.017 & {[}0.104, 0.156{]} & $7{\times}10^{-42}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Pairwise SCS--confusion correlation. $r^2$ indicates the fraction of variance in type confusion explained by embedding similarity. Both correlations are modest in magnitude but robust across datasets and embedding models (both $p \ll 0.001$). Confusion rate for each ordered pair $(t_i, t_j)$ is computed as the total bidirectional confusion count $C(t_i{\to}t_j) + C(t_j{\to}t_i)$ divided by the sum of gold frequencies, aggregated over all count conditions; CIs are pair-level percentile bootstrap ($B{=}1{,}000$).}
\label{tab:pairwise}
\end{table}

The correlations are statistically significant but modest: $r^2 \approx 1.3\text{--}1.7\%$. Type-level cluster bootstrap yields wider but still significant CIs (Few-NERD: [0.069, 0.219]; MAVEN: [0.081, 0.185]; $B = 10{,}000$). We omit FewRel: RE's per-instance classification format makes its confusion matrix not directly comparable to span-level confusion rates; the BCS-controlled experiments (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}) provide controlled stratified comparison evidence for RE. Spearman correlations corroborate: $\rho = 0.144\text{--}0.168$ across all pairs, strengthening to $\rho = 0.27\text{--}0.31$ among pairs with nonzero confusion (though conditioning on the dependent variable may inflate this).

The remaining ${\sim}$98\% of variance likely reflects entity frequency, prompt position effects \cite{lost-in-middle-2024}, mention ambiguity, and type definition quality. We frame pairwise SCS as one specific, actionable mechanism---semantic overlap between type labels is directly amenable to schema design---rather than the totality of degradation. Although pairwise $r^2$ is modest, the signal compounds at the batch level into quartile gaps of 3.9--5.3\,pp under typical batching, reaching 31.2\,pp only under extreme-batch construction ($-$25.5\,pp under v2 prompts; typical-batch gap ${\approx}\,2\text{--}4$\,pp, \S\ref{sec:practical_magnitude}).

\subsection{BCS-Controlled Experiments}
\label{sec:bcs_controlled}

We fix the type count and vary BCS to obtain controlled stratified comparison evidence. Our design controls for count and pool composition. However, BCS-defined strata may differ in type frequency, definition quality, or prompt position---confounds we cannot fully rule out. Table~\ref{tab:bcs_controlled} reports FewRel accuracy under low-BCS and high-BCS batches; the within-pool analysis (\S\ref{sec:within_pool}) controls for residual confounds from pool composition.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Schema} & \textbf{Count} & \textbf{Low-BCS} & \textbf{High-BCS} & \textbf{$\Delta$ (pp)} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
\midrule
16-rel & 8 & 88.9\% & 79.8\% & $-$9.1 & --- \\
80-rel & 8 & 82.3\% & 51.0\% & $-$31.2 & {[}17.7, 42.7{]} \\
80-rel & 16 & 82.8\% & 51.6\% & $-$31.2 & {[}22.9, 39.6{]} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{BCS-controlled FewRel accuracy. Low-BCS batches contain maximally dissimilar types; high-BCS batches contain maximally similar types. The 80-relation extreme-batch gap (31.2\,pp) reflects the wider BCS range; regression slopes are consistent across pools ($p = 0.60$; Table~\ref{tab:within_pool}; see \S\ref{sec:practical_magnitude} for typical-batch magnitudes). The 16-relation condition used a single extreme batch per BCS level; bootstrap CIs require multiple batches.}
\label{tab:bcs_controlled}
\end{table}

At 16 relations, the extreme-batch gap is 9.1\,pp; at 80 relations it reaches 31.2\,pp (CI: [17.7, 42.7]), stable at count 16 (CI: [22.9, 39.6]). An intermediate count${}\,=\,40$ evaluation (40-relation subsets sampled from the 80-relation pool) yields consistent gaps ($-$33.3\,pp at count 8, $-$31.8\,pp at count 16), confirming monotonic decline.

\paragraph{Document-level RE (DocRED).}
The effect replicates on DocRED's 96-relation schema: BCS gap $-$23.3\,pp at count 16 (slope $= -0.713$, $\rho = -0.661$, $p = 1.5 \times 10^{-5}$) and $-$17.6\,pp at count 32 (slope $= -0.690$, $\rho = -0.509$, $p = 0.002$). BCS explains 44--53\% of cross-batch variance ($R^2 = 0.525$ at count 16), substantially higher than FewRel ($R^2 = 0.218$), possibly reflecting a wider confusability range in the DocRED schema, though document-level context may also provide richer cues for type confusion. Across all RE settings, BCS slopes range from $-$0.40 to $-$0.71, significant in all conditions with sufficient BCS range.

The larger absolute gap at 80 relations does not reflect stronger per-unit sensitivity; the within-pool analysis below shows slopes are indistinguishable across pools ($p = 0.60$). The 80-relation pool simply spans a wider BCS range, enabling more extreme batches.

\paragraph{Within-pool controlled comparison.}
\label{sec:within_pool}
A potential confound in the cross-scale comparison is that the 16-relation and 80-relation pools contain different types, so the observed gap could reflect pool composition rather than confusability. To control for this, we draw 100 random 16-relation subsets from the 80-relation pool and evaluate under the same BCS-controlled protocol (Table~\ref{tab:within_pool}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrrr}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{4}{c}{\textbf{Regression (BCS $\to$ Accuracy)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{Quartile $\Delta$}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-5} \cmidrule(lr){6-7}
\textbf{Setting} & \textbf{Slope} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{$r$} & \textbf{$p$} & \textbf{$\Delta$ (pp)} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
\midrule
Independent 16-rel ($n{=}100$) & $-$0.509 & [$-$0.686, $-$0.336] & $-$0.467 & ${<}10^{-6}$ & $-$5.31 & [$-$7.44, $-$3.19] \\
80-pool $\to$ 16-sub ($n{=}100$) & $-$0.401 & [$-$0.701, $-$0.069] & $-$0.221 & 0.027 & $-$3.92 & [$-$8.17, $+$0.38] \\
Full 80-rel ($n{=}25$)$^\dagger$ & $+$0.221 & [$-$1.303, $+$1.695] & $+$0.064 & 0.762 & $-$1.04 & [$-$10.12, $+$8.04] \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize Permutation test (16-rel vs.\ 80-pool slopes): $p = 0.598$ (5{,}000 permutations)} \\
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize $^\dagger$BCS range (IQR\,=\,0.033) insufficient for regression; slope not interpretable.} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Three-way BCS--accuracy analysis on FewRel. Slope: OLS of batch accuracy on BCS (bootstrap CIs, $B{=}10{,}000$). $\Delta$: accuracy gap between top- and bottom-quartile BCS batches. See Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_within_pool} for CI interpretation.}
\label{tab:within_pool}
\end{table}

The within-pool slope ($-$0.401, CI excludes zero) is statistically indistinguishable from the independent 16-relation pool ($-$0.509; permutation $p = 0.598$). The smaller quartile gap ($-$3.92\,pp vs.\ $-$5.31\,pp) reflects the narrower BCS range within the 80-relation pool (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_within_pool}). This confirms the 80-relation extreme-batch gap (31.2\,pp) reflects wider BCS range, not stronger per-unit sensitivity.

In the full 80-relation condition, natural BCS variation is insufficient (IQR${}=0.033$) to yield a detectable effect ($n=25$, slope${}=+0.221$, $p=0.762$). This does not invalidate the confusability effect---the within-pool slope is significant ($p=0.027$) when BCS is deliberately varied---but establishes that natural schema sampling at this scale does not produce sufficient BCS contrast for detection.

\paragraph{Practical magnitude.}
\label{sec:practical_magnitude}
For representative batches from the 80-relation pool ($n{=}100$ random 16-type subsets), the typical BCS span (IQR = 0.054) yields an expected accuracy gap of 2.2\,pp (95\% CI: [0.4, 4.0])---roughly 7\% of the 31.2\,pp extreme-batch gap. Quartile comparison confirms: bottom-quartile BCS batches average 82.8\% vs.\ 78.9\% for top-quartile ($\Delta = 3.9$\,pp). The 31.2\,pp figure reflects maximum BCS contrast, not the typical batch experience. Pair-level $r^2{=}1.5\%$ aggregates to batch-level $R^2{=}22$--$53\%$ across datasets, a 14--35$\times$ amplification consistent with averaging over $\sim\binom{k}{2}$ correlated pair effects per batch.

\paragraph{Cross-model validation.}
The effect replicates on Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct: BCS gaps of $-$27.1\,pp (count 8) and $-$23.9\,pp (count 16), reaching 77--87\% of Qwen3-32B's gap. Both models converge to $\approx$51\% under high confusability regardless of count, while divergence is confined to the low-BCS regime---suggesting a task-inherent difficulty floor under high confusability (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_bcs}).

\paragraph{Prompt sensitivity.}
Under the v2 prompt (stricter format constraints), the extreme-batch BCS gap is $-$25.5\,pp (low-BCS 85.2\% vs.\ high-BCS 59.7\%; 95\% CI: [22.4, 28.6]; $n{=}25$; $r = -0.919$; $p < 0.0001$). Compared to v1's $-$31.2\,pp, the effect attenuates modestly ($-$5.7\,pp) but remains highly significant. NER v2 yields consistent batch-level correlations ($r = +0.58$ vs.\ $r = +0.50$ under v1). Prompt design modulates the magnitude but does not eliminate the confusability effect (full analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompt_sensitivity}).

\subsection{Multi-Channel Degradation}
\label{sec:multi_channel}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_error_taxonomy.pdf}
\caption{Error taxonomy decomposition. IntraSub scales monotonically with BCS in both tasks. RE missed rates remain flat (10--16\%), but NER missed rates are substantially higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic.}
\label{fig:error_taxonomy}
\end{figure}

Error taxonomy decomposition (Table~\ref{tab:error_taxonomy} and Figure~\ref{fig:error_taxonomy}) shows intra-batch substitution (IntraSub) scales monotonically with BCS: $\rho = 1.0$ ($p < 0.001$) for NER and $\rho = 0.954$ ($p < 10^{-6}$) for RE. Under closed-set classification, non-\texttt{none} errors are necessarily intra-batch; the more informative finding is that IntraSub confusion frequency correlates with pairwise SCS ($\rho{=}0.954$, $p{<}0.001$). In NER, missed rates are higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic, indicating confusable types impair entity detection itself. Extreme-BCS batches produce $9.9\text{--}27.5\times$ more confusion than low-BCS batches.

These results establish two channels: \textbf{Channel~1} (count $\to$ recall decline) and \textbf{Channel~2} (confusability $\to$ type confusion), cleanly separable in RE but coupled in NER where type ambiguity and boundary ambiguity interact.

\subsection{Centrality Confound on Homogeneous Schemas}
\label{sec:centrality_confound}

Per-type analysis on MAVEN reveals an anomalous \emph{positive} BCS--F1 correlation (Qwen3 $\rho = +0.184$, $p = 0.025$; MiniLM $\rho = +0.208$, $p = 0.011$). This traces to same-domain embedding collapse \cite{cai-isotropy-clusters-2021}: MAVEN's FrameNet-derived types cluster tightly, so mean BCS conflates confusability with centrality---high-BCS types are semantically central and easy to extract. Controlling for log frequency and degree centrality, the positive correlation vanishes ($p > 0.2$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_residualized_bcs}). The pairwise SCS signal remains intact ($r = 0.129$, $p < 10^{-10}$; Table~\ref{tab:pairwise}); the problem is per-type aggregation, not the underlying relationship. Few-NERD's cross-domain schema does not exhibit this confound ($\rho = -0.293$, $p = 0.02$). Mean BCS is thus reliable only for cross-domain schemas; same-domain schemas require pairwise SCS or centrality adjustment.

